   {:label "(b)",
    :body
    "In this and the following parts, assume that $G$ is a finite\nsubgroup of the group of ring automorphisms that are identity on $F$,\n$\\mathrm{Aut}_F(F[x_1,\\ldots ,x_n])$.\n\nShow that $g(\\alpha)\\in F[x_1,\\ldots ,x_n]$ is a prime element if and\nonly if $\\alpha$ is."}
   {:label "(c)",
    :body
    "We say that two prime elements $\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\in F[x_1,\\ldots ,x_n]$\nare distinct if and only if there is no unit $u$ such that\n$u\\pi_1=\\pi_2$.  Also from now on we assume $G$ is $F$-pure.\n\nShow that if $\\pi$ is prime then either $g(\\pi)=\\pi$ or $g(\\pi)$ and\n$\\pi$ are distinct primes."}
   {:label "(d)",
    :body
    "Assume $\\pi\\in F[x_1,\\ldots x_n]$ is a prime element. Let\n$G(\\pi)$ be the product of the distinct primes of the form $g(\\pi)$.\n\nShow that $G(\\pi)$ is fixed by $G$. If $\\alpha\\in F[x_1,\\ldots ,x_n]$\nis fixed by $G$ and $\\pi$ divides $\\alpha$, show that $G(\\pi)$ divides\n$\\alpha$."}
   {:label "(e)",
    :body
    "Assume that $\\pi$ is a prime element of $F[x_1,\\ldots ,x_n]$. \nShow that $G(\\pi)$ is a prime element of the fixed ring $F[x_1,\\ldots\nx_n]^G=\\{f\\in F[x_1,\\ldots ,x_n]\\pipe \\forall g\\in G:g(f)=f\\}$."}],
