  [{:label "(a)",
    :body
    "Show that $I$ is the largest subset of $R$ which is an ideal in\n$T$. That is, $I$ is an ideal in $T$ and $I$ contains any subset of\n$R$ which is an ideal of $T$."}
   {:label "(b)",
    :body "Show that if $I\\nsubseteq P$ then $Q_1=Q_2$."}
   {:label "(c)",
    :body
    "Let $Q$ be any prime ideal of $T$ with $Q\\cap R=P$ and let $u\\in\nQ$. Consider an arbitrary element in the ring $R[u]$. Of course, such\nan element has the form $a_nu^n+a_{n-1}u^{n-1}+\\ldots +a_1u+a_0$,\nwhere the coefficients $a_i$ come from $R$. \n\nShow that this element is in $Q\\cap R[u]$ if and only if $a_0\\in P$."}
   {:label "(d)",
    :body
    "Returning to $Q_1$ and $Q_2$, pick $u\\in Q_1\\cap Q_2$ and show\nthat $Q_1\\cap R[u]=Q_2\\cap R[u]$."}
   {:label "(e)",
    :body
    "Now suppose that $Q_1\\neq Q_2$. Also suppose that for any ring\n$S$ with $R\\subset S\\subset T$ that $Q_1\\cap S\\neq Q_2\\cap S$\n\\emph{except} when $S=R$. Obviously when $S=R$ the two intersections\nare equal as they both equal $P$. Show under these hypotheses that\n$P=Q_1\\cap Q_2=I$."}],
