    :body
    "There is a compact smooth $n$-(sub)manifold with boundary \n$Y$ in $X$ such that $A\\subset Y$ and $\\partial Y$ separates $A$ and\n$B$. Hint: you may use without proof the well-known theorem that an\narbitrary continuous function $f:X\\rightarrow \\R$ is\n$\\epsilon$-approximated by a smooth map $g_{\\epsilon}$ for any\n$\\epsilon>0$ (\\ie\\ $\\forall x\\in X: |f(x)-g_{\\epsilon}(x)|<\\epsilon$)."}
   {:label "(b)",
    :body
    "Let $\\omega$ be any closed $(n-1)$-form on $X\\setminus (A\\cup\nB)$. We can compute a number $N_{\\omega}=\\int{\\partial Y}\\omega$ where\n$Y$ inherits an orientation from $X$ and $\\partial Y$ has the boundary\norientation. Show that $N_{\\omega}$ is independent of the choice of\n$Y$. Hint: $Y$ can be chosen to lie in an arbitrarily small\nneighborhood of $A$. Why?"}
   {:label "(c)",
    :body
    "Let $f:X\\rightarrow X$ be a map with fixed point $x$. If $+1$ is\nnot an eigenvalue of $df_x:T_x(X)\\rightarrow T_x(X)$, then $x$ is a\n\\emph{Lefschetz fixed point of $f$}, and $f$ is a \\emph{Lefschetz map}\nif all of its fixed points are Lefschetz. Prove that $X$ is compact\nand $f$ is Lefschetz, then $f$ has only finitely many fixed points."}],
